    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d118">

                <pb facs="0100" n="96" sameAs="frus:frus1938v04/pg96"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">693.001/411a: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in Japan</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Grew</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-11-22T19:00:00-05:00">November 22, 1938—7
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>397. At the press conference today the Secretary, in reply to a question relating
                    to the Japanese reply<anchor n="35a" xml:id="d118-f1"/> to our note of October
                        6,<anchor n="36" xml:id="d118-f2"/> stated that, of course, it would be
                    apparent to the correspondents that the Japanese reply is not responsive to the
                    position of this Government as set forth in our note of October 6 and to the
                    general position which this country has consistently taken, throughout our
                    history, with regard to American rights and interests not only in relations with
                    China but in relations with all countries. Beyond this, the Secretary said, he
                    preferred not to comment until the Department should have had more time to study
                    the substance and implications of the Japanese note.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
